#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166701 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "870077e3-99f9-52b9-ac39-26bbc3827466" :question/math-academy-id "e-9035" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\ln(x + y).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z= x + y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z'(x) = f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x + y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 1 + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = \\ln z+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z) = \\ln z + 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166702 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "d3145755-b414-57e5-8039-04ceb1a3a5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9037" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x + y.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y) = x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz = x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 1 + \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form $z'(x) = f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = z+1.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{z+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{z+1}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|z+1| & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\ln{|x+y+1|}  = x + C\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y) = \\ln{|x+y+1|}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cf0dac3-4d13-5a2d-aa90-8c808e699149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 - 3z^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163433} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ae1205-4a7e-5718-9583-f6be0276a73a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z^{2} + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163434} {:answer/id #uuid "0b9e4fac-b2f0-5352-84e4-be868e42015a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 - z^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163435} {:answer/id #uuid "71029930-18eb-5cde-99b5-81a391550102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 - z^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163436} {:answer/id #uuid "75f39f26-5b0b-5df6-9d3c-cb5d05529e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1 - 2z^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163437}] :answer-field/id #uuid "093657e8-681a-5676-ae66-c18d57c5ed27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163432}] :question/id #uuid "f8deb854-1ac2-5d99-9d29-3afca56811fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-107786" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = (2x - 3y)^{2}$.\nUsing a substitution of the form $z = 2x - 3y$, transform the differential equation into an equation of the form $z^{′}(x) = f(z)$. What is the function $f(z)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "541bd680-2f74-58f2-acd0-641bbe38f1d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{z+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241935} {:answer/id #uuid "5608d1ec-02c7-53d4-9bb6-a82a0192e759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{z}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241955} {:answer/id #uuid "89a733fb-19df-57d9-99b6-ccdc043ce3ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{z}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242391} {:answer/id #uuid "9a61b25a-ae9c-5664-8bf2-04704ce45070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{z+5}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242529} {:answer/id #uuid "cce85f44-267b-5248-a4f7-c8742520e8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-5{e}^{z+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a61b25a-ae9c-5664-8bf2-04704ce45070" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{z+5}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242529} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8b85e2a-be80-5ab9-ba75-e0dd0adbbf66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d5d4c4f2-83ab-5c6a-b2e1-62527a7b057c" :question/math-academy-id "q-107797" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x+2y+5}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+2y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{z+5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2{e}^{z+5}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=2{e}^{z+5}+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166851 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29a290a2-bc9e-57bc-84f5-a21cba4848de" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a Simpler Form Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ce51685-f180-59a8-8398-4f2461a0c7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-3{tan}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241867} {:answer/id #uuid "871089d9-f341-5092-8ccb-710312c1500d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-{tan}^{2}⁡(z-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242363} {:answer/id #uuid "87f2e6b6-aaad-513d-a404-4edede8cf981" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{tan}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242373} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf535e8-89f8-5e4c-8b07-ecd2d591cfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242676} {:answer/id #uuid "d418acdd-f22e-5d57-9607-c556cfe678d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+5\\operatorname{tan}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaf535e8-89f8-5e4c-8b07-ecd2d591cfa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242676} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b0aca84-3c6c-5519-bd2a-14b78a11c9d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e81268a9-ddfd-5f92-b961-b0aa841e7817" :question/math-academy-id "q-108802" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={tan}^{2}⁡(3x-5y).\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=3x-5y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=3x-5y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-5\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={tan}^{2}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=3-5{tan}^{2}⁡z.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c51cf0-b1a9-508f-89fe-cf5db654d586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x-2y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241397} {:answer/id #uuid "6cd7770d-b314-5404-b240-b5cfbc07fc7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x+y}(1-x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242156} {:answer/id #uuid "7e0c61fc-9d78-5132-bd22-6bbf13fd939d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x-y}(x+y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242285} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4df47b-1754-5c8f-aba3-53a7f538334e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242566} {:answer/id #uuid "f985d26b-0954-56c4-8beb-06d60810a431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x+y}(x+y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e0c61fc-9d78-5132-bd22-6bbf13fd939d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-x-y}(x+y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242285} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1be1d49-45f2-5b7f-a18f-ad5c635c720b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1afbf5f-1cd8-5fe7-a308-11eeef08401b" :question/math-academy-id "q-108841" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{x+y}-x-y}{x+y}.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=C-x,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =\\frac{{e}^{z}-z}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{z}}{z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z}{{e}^{z}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int z{e}^{-z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing integration by parts on the left side,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=z & \\,⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{-z} & \\,⟹\\,v=-{e}^{-z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int z{e}^{-z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -z{e}^{-z}+\\int {e}^{-z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -z{e}^{-z}-{e}^{-z} & =x+C \\\\ -{e}^{-z}(z+1) & =x+K \\\\ {e}^{-z}(z+1) & =C-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n{e}^{-x-y}(x+y+1)=C-x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)={e}^{-x-y}(x+y+1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166852 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35df900-489d-5f8e-9d9e-075352035790" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Linear Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c6a07b-8faf-5324-b53c-a2c58ed30c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{3x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272464} {:answer/id #uuid "1d7cc5e8-497a-5d6f-aec1-4ad07e072b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{y-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272649} {:answer/id #uuid "28394a0c-f284-5ba2-9531-bf15a477e453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272742} {:answer/id #uuid "a53691e1-9247-57ee-a1ef-92baf61ae110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{y-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273815} {:answer/id #uuid "ede43097-d715-52fa-8f73-02118dc5234a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{3x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28394a0c-f284-5ba2-9531-bf15a477e453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x-y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272742} :answer-field/id #uuid "d44bbdb7-74e5-579b-a1f0-5dcb97d86e01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2be2a98-4693-5e20-8a16-811d290d52b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-149721" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-{e}^{y-3x}.\n$$\n\n \nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=y-3x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+3 & =3-{e}^{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{e}^{z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-z}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int {e}^{-z}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ {e}^{-z} & =x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x-y}=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x,y)={e}^{3x-y}.$"}]}
